    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p127-q71-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "four solutions.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p127-q71-a-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "0<k<1",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p127-q71-a-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Each piece contributes two solutions.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Count the intersections with the horizontal line $y=k$, retaining each piece's domain restriction.\n\nFor $x<0$,\n$$\\begin{align*}|x+1|&=k\\\\x+1&=\\pm k\\\\x&=-1\\pm k.\\end{align*}$$\nThese roots are distinct and both negative exactly when $0<k<1$.\n\nFor $x\\ge0$,\n$$\\begin{align*}2|x-1|-1&=k\\\\|x-1|&=\\frac{k+1}{2}\\\\x&=1\\pm\\frac{k+1}{2}.\\end{align*}$$\nThese roots are distinct and both nonnegative exactly when $-1<k\\le1$.\n\nEach piece contributes at most two solutions. Both contribute two precisely when $\\boxed{0<k<1}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 12
      },
      {
        "id": "p127-q71-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "three solutions.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p127-q71-b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "k=0\\text{ or }k=1",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p127-q71-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "At each level, the first piece contributes one solution and the second contributes two.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "The first piece has two intersections for $0<k<1$, one for $k=0$ or $k\\ge1$, and none for $k<0$. The second piece has two for $-1<k\\le1$, one for $k=-1$ or $k>1$, and none for $k<-1$.\n\nAt $k=0$, the first piece gives $x=-1$. For the second piece,\n$$\\begin{align*}2|x-1|-1&=0\\\\|x-1|&=\\frac12\\\\x&=\\frac12\\text{ or }\\frac32.\\end{align*}$$\nAll three roots satisfy their respective restrictions.\n\nAt $k=1$, the first piece gives $x=-2$; its other candidate $x=0$ is excluded because this piece requires $x<0$. For the second piece,\n$$\\begin{align*}2|x-1|-1&=1\\\\|x-1|&=1\\\\x&=0\\text{ or }2.\\end{align*}$$\nHere $x=0$ is included by the second piece. Thus there are three solutions exactly when $\\boxed{k=0\\text{ or }k=1}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 12
      },
      {
        "id": "p127-q71-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "two solutions.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p127-q71-c-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "-1<k<0\\text{ or }k>1",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p127-q71-c-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "In the first interval, only the second piece contributes; above 1, each piece contributes one.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "For $-1<k<0$, the first piece has no solution because $|x+1|\\ge0$. The second piece gives\n$$x=1\\pm\\frac{k+1}{2}.$$\nBoth roots are distinct and nonnegative, so there are two solutions.\n\nFor $k>1$, the first piece contributes only $x=-1-k$: the other candidate $-1+k$ is positive and fails $x<0$. The second piece contributes only $x=1+\\frac{k+1}{2}$: the other candidate $1-\\frac{k+1}{2}$ is negative and fails $x\\ge0$. There are again two solutions.\n\nAt the boundary levels $k=-1,0,1$, the counts are respectively $1,3,3$. For $0<k<1$ there are four, and for $k<-1$ there are none. Hence $\\boxed{-1<k<0\\text{ or }k>1}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 12
      },
      {
        "id": "p127-q71-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "one solution.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p127-q71-d-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "k=-1",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p127-q71-d-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Only the vertex of the second piece lies at this level.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "The first piece has minimum value $0$ at $x=-1$. The second has minimum value $-1$ at $x=1$.\n\nAt $k=-1$, the first piece has no solution, while\n$$\\begin{align*}2|x-1|-1&=-1\\\\|x-1|&=0\\\\x&=1.\\end{align*}$$\nThe value $x=1$ satisfies $x\\ge0$. Every level above $-1$ has at least two intersections in total, and levels below $-1$ have none. Therefore $\\boxed{k=-1}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 12
      },
      {
        "id": "p127-q71-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "no solutions.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p127-q71-e-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "k<-1",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p127-q71-e-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The minimum value of the function is −1.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "For $x<0$, $|x+1|\\ge0$. For $x\\ge0$, $2|x-1|-1\\ge-1$, with equality at $x=1$.\n\nThe second piece attains every value from $-1$ upwards: for any $k\\ge-1$, the root $x=1+\\frac{k+1}{2}$ is at least $1$ and satisfies its domain restriction. Thus the range of $f$ is $[-1,\\infty)$.\n\nThe horizontal line $y=k$ has no intersection with the graph exactly when $\\boxed{k<-1}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 12
      }
    ]
